msgstr ""
"![Output ng nakaraang code cell](/learning/images/modules/computer-"
"science/qft/extracted-outputs/198a4223-96ab-475e-a83c-75596bf569cb-0.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As you can see, we measure the populations of each state to be more or less "
"equal, give or take some experimental and statistical noise. So, if you take"
" the QFT of a single computational basis state, the result is an equal "
"superposition of all states. If you're familiar with Fourier transforms, "
"this probably doesn't surprise you. One basic principle that can help us "
"build an intuitive connection between a function and its Fourier transform "
"is that the width of a function is inversely proportional to the width of "
"its Fourier transform. So, something that is very localized in time, for "
"example, like a very short pulse, will require a broad range of frequencies "
"to generate that pulse. That signal will be very broad in Fourier space.\n"
msgstr ""
"Tulad ng makikita mo, sinusukat natin ang mga populasyon ng bawat estado na "
"halos pantay, may kaunting experimental at statistical na ingay. Kaya, kung "
"kukuha ka ng QFT ng isang solong computational basis state, ang resulta ay "
"isang pantay na superposition ng lahat ng estado. Kung pamilyar ka sa "
"Fourier transform, hindi ka malamang magugulat dito. Ang isang pangunahing "
"prinsipyo na makakatulong sa atin na bumuo ng intuitive na koneksyon sa "
"pagitan ng isang function at ng Fourier transform nito ay ang lapad ng isang"
" function ay inversely proportional sa lapad ng Fourier transform nito. "
"Kaya, ang isang bagay na napaka-localized sa oras, halimbawa, tulad ng isang"
" napakaikling pulse, ay mangangailangan ng malawak na hanay ng mga dalas "
"upang mabuo ang pulse na iyon. Ang signal na iyon ay magiging napakalawak sa"
" Fourier space.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This fact is actually related to quantum uncertainty! Heisenberg's "
"uncertainty principle is typically stated as $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\ge "
"\\hbar / 2 $. So if the uncertainty in $x$ ($\\Delta x$) is small, the "
"uncertainty in momentum ($\\Delta p$) must be big, and vice versa. It turns "
"out that transforming from the position basis $x$ to the momentum basis $p$ "
"is accomplished through a Fourier transform.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang katotohanang ito ay talagang may kaugnayan sa quantum uncertainty! Ang "
"uncertainty principle ni Heisenberg ay karaniwang isinasaad bilang $\\Delta "
"x \\Delta p \\ge \\hbar / 2 $. Kaya kung ang uncertainty sa $x$ ($\\Delta "
"x$) ay maliit, ang uncertainty sa momentum ($\\Delta p$) ay kailangang "
"malaki, at vice versa. Lumalabas na ang pag-transform mula sa position basis"
" $x$ patungo sa momentum basis $p$ ay nagagawa sa pamamagitan ng Fourier "
"transform.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note: Keep in mind, we're measuring populations in each of the basis states,"
" so we're losing information about the relative phases between the various "
"parts of the superposition. So, while the QFT of any single computational "
"basis state will yield the same even spread in population over all the basis"
" states, the *phases* won't necessarily be the same.\n"
msgstr ""
"Tandaan: Huwag kalimutan, sinusukat natin ang mga populasyon sa bawat isa sa"
" mga basis state, kaya nawawala ang impormasyon tungkol sa mga kamag-anak na"
" phase sa pagitan ng iba't ibang bahagi ng superposition. Kaya, habang ang "
"QFT ng anumang solong computational basis state ay magbubunga ng parehong "
"pantay na pagkalat ng populasyon sa lahat ng basis state, ang mga *phase* ay"
" hindi kinakailangang magiging pareho.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two computational basis states {#two-computational-basis-states}"
msgstr "Dalawang computational basis state {#two-computational-basis-states}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, let's see what happens when we prepare a superposition of computational"
" basis states. What do you think the Fourier transform will look like in "
"this case?\n"
msgstr ""
"Ngayon, tingnan natin kung ano ang mangyayari kapag naghanda tayo ng "
"superposition ng mga computational basis state. Ano sa tingin mo ang hitsura"
" ng Fourier transform sa kasong ito?\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Let's choose the superposition:\n"
msgstr "Piliin natin ang superposition:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} (|0\\rangle + |N/2\\rangle) = "
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} (|000...0\\rangle + |100...0\\rangle)$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} (|0\\rangle + |N/2\\rangle) = "
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} (|000...0\\rangle + |100...0\\rangle)$$\n"

